Z||000003||fotofrank||12-14-2001||10:36 PM||||So does any one know for sure what the rocker ratio is for a DUKE? One thread says 1.7 but I seem to remember reading 1.6. What is the answer?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000004||MR2KLR||12-14-2001||10:50 PM||||I to have also heard diffrent ratios being stock I thought 1.5 was stock I then read in this post 1.6 was stock and then I heard 1.7 are stock in my post on rockers in the mall. Did they change ratios at some point?<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||CHUCKVH||12-15-2001||02:24 AM||||I don't know about earlier years, but my 88 factory shop manual says the ratio is 1.75:1!<P>Please note that the actual effective ratio of whatever you install may vary due to the valve train geometry when installed.  Also, custom length pushrods may be required with any cam, lifter, or rocker change.  And don't forget to check valve to piston clearances during assembly!  A couple of good books on high performance engine assembly are worth their weight in gold for anyone doing custom work to their engine.  I have been building stock, high performance, supercharged and turbocharged engines for about 29 years now, and I still refer to all the books and magazines, as well as always keeping my eyes open for new ones.<P>Chuck||||reg||1
Z||000006||fotofrank||12-15-2001||02:57 PM||||Thanks CHUCKVH. Now, does anyone have a shop manual for the '84 - '86 Duke. I really want to know  the stock ratio before I commit to anything. I also want to contact a manufacturer before I buy just to see what they say and have in stock.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||ITB 2M4||12-16-2001||12:12 AM||||OK, according to my 1984 Pontiac Fiero FACTORY Sevice Manual, it's 1.75 to 1.<P>That's on page 6A-35, Figure 6A-68<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000008||Blacktree||12-16-2001||12:31 AM||||Well, I don't know how reliable this info is, but according to my P.A.W. catalog the Crane Gold Race roller rockers for the 2.5 engine have a 1.71:1 ratio, and it says that's the stock ratio for 1977-1989. But then again it also says the 2.5 liter engine displaces 161 cubic inches.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fotofrank||12-16-2001||12:03 PM||||I agree 1.75:1 is the correct ratio for an iron Duke. <P>This is an excerpt from SuperChevy mag:<P>   "if you are runing a very mild engine and do not worry about power above 4500rpm you can reduce the rocker arm ratio.It doesn't sound right but it will incress low end power. You won't get as much high end power as with incressed ratio rockers but if yor engine never sees that rpm why bother?"<P>This seems to mean that changing from 1.75 to 1.70 ratio rockers the low end gets a boost. After all what rpm do you hit going from stop light to stop light and what is the peak rpm for a Duke?<BR>I don't think I want to go to a ratio lower than 1.7o with rollers.<p>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
